# Avatar

A circular identity marker. Basecoat ships no component — an avatar is
an `<img>` styled with utilities, written inline: `rounded-full` for the
circle, `object-cover` to fill it, `size-*` for the dimensions.

<%= embed :avatar, :default %>

```html
<img class="size-10 shrink-0 object-cover rounded-full"
     alt="Jane Smith" src="/avatars/jane-smith.jpg">
```

## When to use

- Marking a person in lists, headers and menus — patients, users,
  clinicians.
- Anywhere a row of person-records needs a fast visual anchor.

## When not to use

- Organisations, teams or systems — use the entity's lucide icon
  (`building`, `users`), not a fake person marker.
- Decoration — an avatar always stands for a specific identity.
- Initials when there is no image — that is the shell's `user-avatar`
  treatment (header user menu, patient info header), not this `<img>`.

## Anatomy and options

Just an `<img>` plus utilities: `rounded-full` (or `rounded-lg` for a
squared avatar), `object-cover`, and a `size-*` for the dimensions
(`size-8` in dense rows, `size-14` in a profile header). Overlap a row
with `flex -space-x-2` and a ring per image.

## Do and don't

- Do set `alt` to the person's name when the avatar isn't directly
  beside the visible name — it is the accessible identity.
- Do keep one size per context (small in dense rows, large in profile
  headers); don't vary size for emphasis.
- Don't stack avatars into overlapping piles as a count — say the
  number; piles hide identities, which is the avatar's one job.

## Accessibility and content

- `alt` carries the identity. When the adjacent text already names the
  person (the patient header pattern), the avatar is decorative — use
  `alt=""` so assistive tech skips it.
